Sure there is.  Given x potions of one and y potions of another,
there will be some optimal strategy of the size of stacks of one
to dip into another to maximize the number of alchemized potions
you'll end up with.

For instance, if x is 30 and y is 3, then dipping all of your 
potions of x at once is obviously a bad idea; it will never work.
On the other hand, dip them 10 at a time and you've got about a
60% chance of making 30 potions of (x+y).  I think that's still
way too high.

